 Added more tests for tel since in monkey testing I realised it actually breaks. Will attempt to fix. 

Specifically tel:xxxx will break if tel:xxxx is not preceded by a plus, and is more than 65536. > Added more tests for tel since in monkey testing I realised it actually breaks. Will attempt to fix.

okay

> Specifically tel:xxxx will break if tel:xxxx is not preceded by a plus, and is more than 65536.

Oooh. Crazy. > The text for open default browser makes no sense. We could update it to "Open default browser":

I'll change it in the following commit.

> Do you think it will confuse the users that default ports (443, 80, 21, ...) are cut of:

~~Maybe we should avoid changing the user's input? Not just less confusing but also less likely to have breaking issues. Additionally, if I'm not wrong this can be done by just removing two lines of code.~~

Edit: No, the default behaviour for UriBuilder is to ignore the port number, it seems. Not sure how it can be changed. Even when explicitly declaring port in UriBuilder, when the UriBuilder.toString() is called the port will be dropped from the string conversion. > > Do you think it will confuse the users that default ports (443, 80, 21, ...) are cut of:
> 
> ~Maybe we should avoid changing the user's input? Not just less confusing but also less likely to have breaking issues. Additionally, if I'm not wrong this can be done by just removing two lines of code.~
> 
> Edit: No, the default behaviour for UriBuilder is to ignore the port number, it seems. Not sure how it can be changed. Even when explicitly declaring port in UriBuilder, when the UriBuilder.toString() is called the port will be dropped from the string conversion.

I think we can leave it as is. The protocol changes based on port, so the functionality isn't affected.
- mySite:80 => http://mySite
- mySite:443 => https://mySite
- ftp://myFTP:21 => ftp://myFTP
Thank you for your contribution so far. If the marked things are fixed I can test it on a local build. Code looks good to me. Only a small improvement suggestion left. Functionality works as expected.

review_comments:
This will ignore all the versions installed on the computer. So If we have Code, Code-Insiders and/or VSCodium all will have  by default the path of the portable vscode version. So my recomendation is validation  before the foreach. if a folder exists in the enviroment variables with that structure, add it as a path. And then if the folder is of type portable put the AppData of the portable version.
![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/20097801/179366937-204c9abf-d745-43ce-a5fa-79b929067ffe.png)
@bkmeneguello  Actually this doesn't break functionality of installed instances as there is no 'data' dir in their iconPath. However, this requires having `vscode_portable_dir/bin` in PATH env var to work. I think we should at least note this somewhere You are right. For me this is ok then.  Lets put the PATH warning in the subheader text and docs as well. The warning here is that "Portable instances of of VSCode need to in the PATH environment variable" work, right @stefansjfw ? Correct. Because VS Code plugin goes through PATH env var and looks for executable and related data

