That reminds me of a discussion about `MAX_PATH` [here](https://github.com/microsoft/PowerToys/pull/884#discussion_r355341906). 
[The docs](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/fileio/naming-a-file) do not list `GetModuleFileName` as a function with a path length restrictions lifted if you opt-in to long path behavior, but [`GetModuleFileName` docs](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/libloaderapi/nf-libloaderapi-getmodulefilenamew) state that it's always possible to get a long path from it:
> `Therefore, the path can be a long or short file name, and can use the prefix "\?"`

Maybe we should write a wrapper for the function for people to use, since it seems like a common op to do, e.g.:
```cpp
std::wstring get_module_filename(HMODULE mod = nullptr)
{
  wchar_t buffer[MAX_PATH + 1];
  DWORD actual_length = GetModuleFileNameW(mod, buffer, MAX_PATH);
  if(GetLastError() == ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER)
  {
    const DWORD long_path_length = 0xFFFF; // should be always enough
    std::wstring long_filename(long_path_length, L'\0');
    actual_length = GetModuleFileNameW(mod, long_filename.data(), long_path_length);
    return long_filename.substr(0, actual_length);
  }
  return {buffer, actual_length};
}
