`OnImport` always happen on the PS pipeline thread, so you can do the following instead, which is way more efficient than opening a new `Runspace`.
```
using (var pwsh = PowerShell.Create(RunspaceMode.CurrentRunspace))
{
    var results = pwsh.AddCommand("Get-Command")
        .AddParameter("Name", "winget")
        .AddParameter("CommandType", "Application")
        .Invoke();

    if (restuls.Count is 0)
    {
        return;
    }
}
``` It looks you need to refresh the catalog from time to time. It would be better to have a data structure wraping the auto-refreshment of the catalog in some pre-defined intervals. The interval can be made configurable through a cmdlet exposed by this module.

With that, you won't need to call `connect()` when the feedback provider is triggered. It's possible that the provider is triggered while the connect refreshing is in progress, in which case the `_FindPackages` will wait for the refreshment to be done. But more likely, refreshment is not in progress when the feedback provider is being triggered, and thus it would be way faster for the feedback provider to return. > There's still an issue where the feedback provider won't work for the first 2 to 3 seconds.

Which one takes more time: the initialization of `WinGetComObjects.Singleton`, or the `connect()` call?

For the `WinGetComObjects` initialization, you should be able to do it in `OnImport()`, on a thread-pool thread. Or, this can be done in the constructor of `WinGetCommandNotFoundFeedbackPredictor` -- call a method to create the singleton of `WinGetComObjects` on a background thread.

In that way, you won't block the module load, but is able to kick off the initialization in the background, so when the first trigger comes, the initialization is likely already done. This could be made a const instead.
```suggestion
        private const byte Max_Suggestions = 5;
``` Also, by looking at the usage of `_maxSuggestions`, it's probably better to make it the `int` type, so as to avoid some implicit conversation. Given `WinGetCommandNotFoundFeedbackPredictor` doesn't implement `IDisposable`, is this method really needed? Now the `IFeedbackProvider` has a `Trigger` property whose default implementation is `FeedbackTrigger.CommandNotFound`

https://github.com/PowerShell/PowerShell/blob/3f3d79d4758704c8dad5ca7c12690ba62fd03a3b/src/System.Management.Automation/engine/Subsystem/FeedbackSubsystem/IFeedbackProvider.cs#L217-L223

So, with the default trigger, you no longer need to do this check. Here is the code from `command-not-found` module as an example for you: https://github.com/PowerShell/command-not-found/blob/4c9697c94eff24cd8d04cff91eca213d08f931eb/src/FeedbackProvider.cs#L56-L58 Please don't create a new PowerShell instance every time `FindPackage` is called. You should have a pool of them, and checkout 1 for a call to `FindPackage`. There could be different strategies for the pool's behavior, the simplest would be fixed number of objects in the pool, and when the pool is empty, `FindPackages` simply returns null.

Creation of the pool should happen in a background thread when the module gets loaded. You may want to have the `Microsoft.WinGet.Client` module imported when creating a PowerShell instance, and this can be done by the following code:
```c#
var iss = [initialsessionstate]::CreateDefault2();
iss.ImportPSModule(new[] { "Microsoft.WinGet.Clinet" });
var ps = PowerShell.Create(iss);
// Insert the PowerShell object to the pool.
``` You need to call `ps.Commands.Clear()` before running the next command. Otherwise, the behavior will be like `Find-WinGetPackage | Find-WinGetPackage` For those duplicate parameter settings, you can do the following:
```c#
var common = new Hashtable()
{
    ["MatchOption"] = "StartsWithCaseInsensitive",
    ["Count"] = 5,
    ["Source"] = "winget"
};

ps.AddParameter("Command", query)
  .AddParameters(common)
  .Invoke();
``` Consider change the return type to `Collection<PSObject>`. The use of the returned object doesn't require any list specific features, so it's better to avoid the `ToList` cast.

```suggestion
        private Collection<PSObject> FindPackages(string query, ref bool tooManySuggestions, ref string packageMatchFilterField)
``` I recommend you to implement the `ICommandPredictor` interface as well, so that when a user starts typing `winget` as shown in the suggested actions in the feedback result, prediction will keep in to complete the command based on the suggested actions.

Here is an example of that experience based on the `command-not-found` utility on the Linux systems:
https://github.com/PowerShell/command-not-found/blob/main/tools/images/FeedbackProvider.gif

The code can be found at https://github.com/PowerShell/command-not-found/blob/main/src/FeedbackProvider.cs#L9, which you can use as an example to implement the `ICommandPredictor` interface along with `IFeedbackProvider`. Cool! How do I trigger the `ICommandPredictor`? I've been trying to test it out and <kbd>Tab</kbd> won't retrieve the list, it seems. Consider using a pool for the PowerShell object. See the comment https://github.com/microsoft/PowerToys/pull/26319#discussion_r1319124929 for details. It's not tab completion, so `Tab` won't work by default. Please take a look at this doc about how the predictor works with PSReadLine:
https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/powershell/scripting/learn/shell/using-predictors?view=powershell-7.3
Let me know if you still cannot get it to show up in the command line. This may result in exception being thrown when `winget` is not installed, as you didn't register them when loading. Perfect! This works exactly as you'd expect and I LOVE it. Thanks! This can be made readonly by looking at the use of it. So this is essentially a single-item pool :)

There is a potential risk of using a single PowerShell object -- if for some reason, the call to `_ps.Invoke()` doesn't return within the timeout limit (PowerShell host calls `GetFeedback` with a timeout limit of 300 ms), and then another call to `GetFeedback` comes in, which calls `_ps.Invoke()` again, then a race condition will happen.

You can evaluate based on your judgement whether this may be a problem for the `Find-WinGetPackage` cmdlets, but be noted that it also depends on the system where this module is running on, say, this may be more likely to happen on a heavy-loaded system because everything runs slowly.

You may mitigate this by checking `_ps.Runspace.RunspaceAvailability == Available` before using `_ps` and simple doing nothing and return when it's not `Available`. But this won't prevent the race condition completely. Maybe you can consider refactoring the parameter passing a bit as shown in the following code

```suggestion
            var options = new Hashtable()
            {
                ["MatchOption"] = "StartsWithCaseInsensitive"
                ["Source"] = "winget",
            };
            
            // 1) Search by command
            _ps.Commands.Clear();
            var pkgList = _ps.AddCommand("Find-WinGetPackage")
                .AddParameter("Command", query)
                .AddParameters(options)
                .Invoke();
            ...
            
            options["MatchOption"] = "ContainsCaseInsensitive";
            
            // 2) No matches found,
            //    search by name
            _ps.Commands.Clear();
            pkgList = _ps.AddCommand("Find-WinGetPackage")
                .AddParameter("Name", query)
                .AddParameters(options)
                .Invoke();
            ....
            
            // 3) No matches found,
            //    search by moniker
            _ps.Commands.Clear();
            pkgList = _ps.AddCommand("Find-WinGetPackage")
                .AddParameter("Moniker", query)
                .AddParameters(options)
                .Invoke();
            ....
``` I'm actually going to leave this as-is for now. It's a bit easier to read, and if we change the parameters (which is totally possible before this PR gets merged), it'll be less of a pain.

Leaving the comment unresolved though, so that I come back to it when the PR gets out of draft. ```suggestion
            var iss = InitialSessionState.CreateDefault2();
``` ```suggestion
using System.Management.Automation;
using System.Management.Automation.Runspaces;
``` It should return `true` only if `ps` is not null. ```suggestion
            // Create the pool and pre-populate the pool with a PowerShell object.
            _pool = ObjectPool.Create(new PooledPowerShellObjectPolicy());
            _pool.Return(_pool.Get());
``` `_pool.Get()` will always return a PowerShell object -- if the pool is not empty, return one from the pool; if the pool is empty, create a new one and return. > `_pool = ObjectPool.Create(new PooledPowerShellObjectPolicy());`

That throws a compiler error: 
```
error CS0310: 'PowerShell' must be a non-abstract type with a public parameterless constructor in order to use it as parameter 'T' in the generic type or method 'ObjectPool.Create<T>(IPooledObjectPolicy<T>?)'
```

That said, I did add the line to prepopulate the pool. Thanks! Any reason to not directly implement `IPooledObjectPolicy<PowerShell>`? This line can be removed now, given that the `WarmUp` method will create the first PowerShell object and warm it up. Nitpick: return null in case there is no result, to reduce the GC need.

