@enricogior what is Your opinion on this one ? Let's keep it simple, we only say "Cannot launch FancyZones editor. Please report etc."
So users that will encounter this bug will report it, otherwise they might just use the hotkey and never report it. OK, I'll also leave a note here, that if we add new custom action (if ever), error message should be modified. It would be better if we can just disable FZ and not exit the runner otherwise it would be impossible to turn FZ off for the user and still use the other modules. That is correct. However I'm not sure about mechanism to signalize from library back to dll to trigger disable. Done. FancyZones disabled in case of unrecoverable error. Callback mechanism is not used still, but I think it is OK to leave it in place for future use. Let's use a better name for this variable. Is this a fatal error? What about using default data? disableCallback -> disableModuleCallback Hm, You're right, now that we have priority grid with 3 zones in it, we could do most of the regular fancyzones functionalities. I'll investigate to see amount of changes needed to have this in place. I assume there will be a need for a lot of if-else statements based on current implementation. What I meant is: if loading data fails, we should not disabled FZ but just ignore/reset the corrupted data. In this case it is not that data within json file is wrong (we handle that properly), it is the fact that we couldn't open the file in the first place. Therefore we cannot load any data, but maybe we can overwrite it with new data, if we retry opening the file again. We should sleep for 1 or 2 seconds and retry, and only after the second failure we should either disable the module or decide to nuke the file and start fresh.
For now let's add a retry and then report the error and disable the module. We don't know if this is happening frequently and if nuking the file is the best approach. Personally, I think that chance of this happening is quite low, but sure, I'll add a retry after a 1sec sleep. Later we can proceed with nuking the file. Best name I came up with.
```
// If non-recoverable error occurs during initialization of FancyZones module, disable
// its core functionality. Accessing FancyZones settings will still be possible.
bool disableFancyZonesModuleCore{ false };
``` Cannot find path to FancyZones persisted data -> Cannot find FancyZones persisted data pah Canno launch FancyZones editor. ->The FancyZones editor failed to start. Actually, it would be better: FancyZones persisted data pah not found. Cannot load FancyZones settings. - > Failed to load the FancyZones settings. Cannot save FancyZones settings. Please retry again later, if problem persists report the bug to
->
**Failed to save the** FancyZones settings. Please retry again later, if **the** problem persists report the bug to Is this needed? If we are calling disable() when setting disableFancyZonesModuleCore, it should set m_app to null? call disable() here instead? Disable will do nothing here as `m_app` is null. This variable is important to make early exit in every subsequent call to `enable()`. This variable is used to make early exit in every call to `enable()`, which essentially comes after interface is constructed. After reviewing the code it seems like we can avoid introducing the `disableFancyZonesModuleCore` and just make sure that `m_app` is null. Calling `Disable(false)` will guarantee that `m_app` is set to null. That is true, but if runner calls `enable` again, we will enable powertoy, even though we shouldn't. This exception is caught by https://github.com/microsoft/PowerToys/blob/85c0eaa5980cabab16089fea3a2c6f148242a368/src/runner/main.cpp#L132-L139
that doesn't report any error, we should add a messagebox there reporting the module name that failed. If it's called again, we try again and in case we fail, not a big problem. I prefer to have less variables that effect the module state.

