Not sure if this is a good idea. Could lead to many results.

 We have a problem 🙅  `query.Search` and `query.RawQuery` always trim the last spaces
(see `PowerLauncher.Plugin.QueryBuilder`)

so I can find out that a user only write `\ `  (backslash with space) to show all values of a key > We have a problem 🙅  `query.Search` and `query.RawQuery` always trim the last spaces
> (see `PowerLauncher.Plugin.QueryBuilder`)
> 
> so I can find out that a user only write `\ `  (backslash with space) to show all values of a key

Don't understand.
What would you expect to find out? Hello,

before the last change when a user type two backslashes `\\` and nothing more, I show all value names of the current selected key.

Now I looking for a backslash and a space and the characters after the space. When a user write `hklm\cons\ color`, I search for first key they start with `cons` in the base key `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` and filter all value names of this key that contain `color`, so far so good.

But, i need a simple concept to show all value names of a key.

And currently I can't figure out that a user have write `hklm\cons\ ` (end with space). I get always only `hklm\cons\` (without a space) and so I show all keys behind the given query key of `hklm\cons\` and not the value inside the key.

 > Hello,
> 
> before the last change when a user type two backslashes `\\` and nothing more, I show all value names of the current selected key.
> 
> Now I looking for a backslash and a space and the characters after the space. When a user write `hklm\cons\ color`, I search for first key they start with `cons` in the base key `HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE` and filter all value names of this key that contain `color`, so far so good.
> 
> But, i need a simple concept to show all value names of a key.
> 
> And currently I can't figure out that a user have write `hklm\cons\ ` (end with space). I get always only `hklm\cons\` (without a space) and so I show all keys behind the given query key of `hklm\cons\` and not the value inside the key.
> 
> 

Can we lock at indexer plugin, how it's done there. The `Indexer Plugin` don't have this problem.
It show all (folders and files) after the backslash or filter the results by a keyword after the backslash

Possible solutions:

1. Introduce extra action keywords (`>` and `*`) similar to
![2020-11-27 08_58_30-PowerLauncher](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/17874713/100425104-e4614000-308e-11eb-9cc5-f961c840318d.png)
Like: `Use > to search within a registry key. Use * to search for values. Or use both >*`
* Pro: No new keywords
* Cons: User must know these keywords
2. Show all sub-keys and value names after the `\`
 * Pro: Same behavior as the file explorer and the index plugin
 * Cons: Not the same behavior as the registry editor
3. Introduce a new property in `Query.cs` that contains the real untouched input
 * Pro: No side effects
 * Cons: Extra property
4. Use a placeholder to show all values of a key like `*`
 * Pro: Use of the typical `Show all` placeholder
 * Cons: A value name can start with have a `*`
 * Cons: Introduce extra syntax that are hard to remove later (When people have got used to it)

I prefer a combination of  1 and 2  
