review_comments:
same issue, this will break settings ui Don't change number values for enums that are already shipped, this will break everyone's settings!
Settings.json stores this as:
`"copiedcolorrepresentation": 0,` - so if you had HEX before, it will be changed to CMYK, what is not expected or intended. New enums should be added at the end, there is no need to keep them in alphabetical order. Why did you add these unnecessary returns (also into each other property?  Next time, please do just functional changes and keep style changes for a separate PR. It is really hard to follow what are real changes and what is just formatting Based on memory, this feels like a suggested resharper tweak. I don't use ReSharper since version 8.0, but its the same idea.

It is to reduce the indentation of code 
and use only (when possible) flow-control on the indentation (e.g. return earlier)

```csharp
            set
            {
                if (_isEnabled == value)
                {
                    return;
                }

                _isEnabled = value;
                OnPropertyChanged(nameof(IsEnabled));

..........

            }
``` I can split this PR into two separate PRs tomorrow, when you need ^^ I understand what it does, but dont see any added value, it is just describing what will compiler do later anyway. Properties should be kept short and this just adds unnecessary lines of code Lets keep it for now I have remove this/change it back  `asynchronous` `brightness` `completely` Oh, `binded` should probably be `bound`. But the sentence also needs work in that case. (It's too late for me to try to figure out what you're trying to say here.) To make the bot not complain about `FEF`, you could add a line to: https://github.com/microsoft/PowerToys/blob/master/.github/actions/spell-check/patterns.txt

e.g.
```code
\[DataRow\("[0-9A-F]{6}", \d{3}, \d{3}, \d{3}\)\]
``` Thanks ok, it turns out you have more than one syntax here... But didn't cover this either My example covered this one. This should be `IRead` -- the spell checker parsed `IReadOnlyDictionary` as `IRead` + `Only` + `Dictionary` and was happy w/ 2/3 of them oh I see, there a more than one pattern needed ^^ You can fix this in various ways...

One approach is to merge the various flavors (you can test w/ a [regex tester](https://regex101.com/), or on your fork in a different branch):
```
\[DataRow\("[0-9A-F]{6}", (?:"[A-Z]\d+"|\d{3}(?:\.\d|))(?:, \d{3}(?:\.\d|)){2}\)\]
```
Or you could have multiple expressions for the individual cases -- I think one expression is reasonable as long as people can more or less read it. (You could also relax it further, but DataRow is used by others in the repository, so I'm not advocating ignoring everyone else's uses of it.) -- I've compressed it a bit because it's a bit repetitive. But it'd depend on who's reviewing it as to whether the `{2}` is a worthwhile compression. Thanks, yes I have notice this Thanks, but i prefer two separate patterns, that make it easier to read

