review_comments:
@enricogior @stefansjfw One possible option, that we should consider is when window is created (`FancyZones::WindowCreated` callback) we do the check if we still have zeroed GUID, and if that is the case we migrate it based on `IVirtualDesktopManager::GetWindowDesktopId` and HWND of that newly created window. Creation of new window is something that will happen right as system is started, which means we would migrate primary desktop data quite quickly. Example:
```
// IFancyZonesCallback
IFACEMETHODIMP_(void)
FancyZones::WindowCreated(HWND window) noexcept
{
    auto& fancyZonesData = FancyZonesDataInstance();
    if (!fancyZonesData.HasValidPrimaryDesktopId())
    {
        GUID desktopId{};
        if (VirtualDesktopUtils::GetWindowDesktopId(window, &desktopId))
        {
            fancyZonesData.UpdatePrimaryDesktopData(desktopId);
        }
    }
    ...
}
```
I'm aware that this method is called quite often, however check `HasValidPrimaryDesktopId` is fairly simple, and code within if statement will be called only once.

issue_comments_text:
Awesome work. Thanks Great idea and work. 👍

Just two questions:
- I am not sure if it is possible to span zones over multiple monitors at the moment. But if so, van the user also select both monitors at the same time?
- How does it behave with Virtual Desktop? > * I am not sure if it is possible to span zones over multiple monitors at the moment. But if so, van the user also select both monitors at the same time?
> * How does it behave with Virtual Desktop?

Thanks! 
In the current implementation, it's not possible to select both monitors at the same time. Spanning zones overs monitors is possible if you use the `Allow zones to span across monitors` option. 
Regarding VD, the temp file with layouts data using by the editor contains layouts only for active VD. This way you can open the editor on any VD and see corresponding data.    @htcfreek 

> I am not sure if it is possible to span zones over multiple monitors at the moment. But if so, van the user also select both monitors at the same time?

If `Allow zones to span across monitors` is on, only one monitor will be available and it will allow to create zones that span across monitors.
We will not implement a feature that will allow to select multiple monitors in the editor.

> How does it behave with Virtual Desktop?

As @SeraphimaZ said, nothing changes compared to the current behavior. If you want to edit the layout for a VD, first switch to that VD and then open the FZ editor. /azp run <samp>
Azure Pipelines successfully started running 1 pipeline(s).<br>

</samp> can't wait to try this out :) On a system with two monitors, put a 6-zone grid layout on one monitor and a 8-zone grid layout on the other, then switch between the two monitors a few times. The displayed preview will get mixed up.

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/9358361/96247770-dc4fb400-0faa-11eb-94af-8f03e8dee938.png)
 > On a system with two monitors, put a 6-zone grid layout on one monitor and a 8-zone grid layout on the other, then switch between the two monitors a few times. The displayed preview will get mixed up.
> 

fixed When using 100% scaling on the left monitor and 125% scaling on the right monitor, zone layouts are not shown where they're supposed to, they're shifted to the left, partially covering the left monitor. The number 2 is clipped

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/9358361/96259543-0ced1900-0fbe-11eb-9247-6bb4ac396a1a.png)
 > When using 100% scaling on the left monitor and 125% scaling on the right monitor, zone layouts are not shown where they're supposed to, they're shifted to the left, partially covering the left monitor.

fixed @SeraphimaZ @ivan100sic 
the UI glitches regarding the "selected desktop" graphics can be fixed later and not block this PR.
There are other changes I would like to make to it, like relative position and DPI scaling, right now the DPI is not taken into account and the monitor is not scaled and also the resolution is always the native resolution. >> When using 100% scaling on the left monitor and 125% scaling on the right monitor, zone layouts are not shown where they're supposed to, they're shifted to the left, partially covering the left monitor.

>fixed

I ran `git clean` and then built the entire solution, but I still have this problem. I deleted my previous comment because it was not accurate. Reposting the problem. It happens when opening the editor on the primary monitor (the one on the right) and then switching to the 4K.

If both monitors are at 100% DPI everything works.

When I set the 4K monitor to 150%, or any other scaling factor > 100%, after I switch to the 4K monitor, the zones are shifted.

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3206696/96269931-4a0bd800-0fcb-11eb-93b4-4d79b64c82ab.png)

But if the primary monitor has also a scaling factor at 125%, and 4K is at 125% or 150%, it works. Not if the primary monitor is at 125% and the 4K at 200%.
So there is a strange relation between scaling factors.

![image](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/3206696/96269865-32345400-0fcb-11eb-9830-c05875184229.png)

Note: I checked the argument passed from C++ and they seem correct. We pass the native resolution and the DPI factor. Same scenario as before, two monitors left one 100% scaling, right one 125% scaling.

When previewing e.g. the Grid zone layout on the left monitor, it's 20 pixels (half of taskbar height) towards the bottom.

When editing a custom zone layout on the second monitor, the zones can go off-screen to the bottom and to the right.

When editing a grid layout, after clicking cancel, the main window is gone but the zones are still shown and you have to quit using (Alt+F4)  /azp run <samp>
Pull request contains merge conflicts.<br>

